---
description: Two-track SDLC, Linear artifact homes, and the four external gates.
alwaysApply: true
---

Internal apps live in the shared monorepo, use the shared shadcn system, and sign in with Google through Cloudflare Access on `rtcs.app` subdomains. Flow: brief, grill, build, pull request with evidence, Bugbot, Robert merges, auto-deploy.

External products start from `effect-modulith-template` in their own repo and their own design system, on an `rtcs.app` subdomain.

1. Entry. Start Cursor from an agreed Linear issue. If none exists, brief Jarvis first.
2. Gate 1 Grill. Grill notes in a Linear document.
3. Gate 2 Product spec. Domain concepts and ubiquitous language in a Linear document.
4. Gate 3 Design doc. One Linear document for the prototype and the C4 spec, approved section by section (`spec-c4`).
5. Gate 4 Build. The pull request body carries the acceptance criteria checklist. Bugbot reviews the diff. The agent answers every finding, at most two fix cycles, then reports findings still open (`ship-pr-evidence`, `verify-module-fsm`).

Linear documents hold specs, grill notes, design docs, and product decisions. Approve each section on its own. Verbal approval in chat tells Jarvis to write `Approved: <date>` under the section heading.

Code ADRs go in `docs/decisions/` in the product repo. `cortex` holds skills, rules, `AGENTS.md`, `products.yaml`, and `docs/linear.md`. A pull request holds executable code.

The Linear workspace model is `docs/linear.md` in `rtcs-dev/cortex`.

An agent merges a pull request only after Robert asks for that specific pull request. A merge to `main` runs checks, database migrations, the Cloudflare deploy, and health checks.
